STABLE
------
== Drop support for PostgreSQL versions before 12
extension_drop depends on cat_tools, which already requires PostgreSQL
12+. A fresh install of extension_drop was already broken below that
too (a cat_tools function it calls needs PG11+ internally) -- this
just makes the declared floor honest.

== Run drop commands when `DROP SCHEMA ... CASCADE` or `DROP OWNED BY` drops an extension
The event trigger now fires for `DROP SCHEMA` and `DROP OWNED` as well as
`DROP EXTENSION`, and returns immediately unless an extension is among the
dropped objects, so other drops are unaffected. Previously the other two
commands skipped the registered SQL and left a stale registration that made
every later `DROP EXTENSION` fail with `XD001`; remove any existing stale
registration with `extension_drop__repair()`.

Dropping an unregistered extension (by any of the three commands) needs only
USAGE on the extension's schema, to resolve the new `SECURITY DEFINER`
function `extension_drop__registered()` (executable by `PUBLIC`), which the
trigger asks which of the dropped extensions are registered; it needs no
privileges on `extension_drop__commands`. Dropping a registered extension
additionally needs SELECT and DELETE on that table. A role without the access
gets an `insufficient_privilege` error that names the table, its owner and
the extensions being dropped, with the underlying error as DETAIL and a HINT
giving the grants that fix it, instead of `permission denied for table
extension_drop__commands` or `type "extension_drop__commands" does not
exist`. The registered SQL runs as the caller and may need further
privileges.

== Remove redundant client_min_messages handling from install script
`CREATE EXTENSION` already raises `client_min_messages` to `WARNING` for
the install script (only-raising, so a stricter caller is respected) and
restores it afterward, so the script's own save/restore was redundant --
and being blind to the caller's level, it could actually lower a
stricter caller's `client_min_messages` during install.

== Mark release as stable
No functional issues turned up in the 9+ years since 0.1.1; promoting
from `testing` to `stable`.

0.1.1
-----
Fix extension dependencies.

0.1.0
-----
Initial release.
